  "query_preview": "..Moisture from tropical cyclones Odalys and Polo is \nbeginning to overspread the area, starting in the high and mid \nlevels at first which has increased cloud cover today, with some \nlight rain trying to reach the surface late this afternoon. The \npotential for showers and thunderstorms will continue to increase \novernight as moisture surges across AZ.\n\nBy Monday, a deep West Coast trough approaches with a closed \nupper-level low expected to develop at its base, then slowly move\nacross the region Tuesday. This trough will act upon the tropical\nmoisture already in place and lift from the approaching trough \nwill generate some embedded thunderstorms that could become strong\nto severe at times with heavy rain, strong winds and some large \nhail. WPC continues to message a moderate risk for excessive \nrainfall for most of the region for Monday and then shifting focus\nfor the eastern part of the state Tuesday as the trough and \nclosed upper-level low move eastward. A Flood Watch remains in \neffect for Monday and Tuesday. Widespread rainfall amounts of 1 to\n2 inches forecast across the high deserts of northeast Arizona \nhave an average return interval of 5 to 10 years, making this at \nleast an uncommon event there. High temperatures across the region\nMonday and Tuesday will be quite cool due to the cloud cover and \nrainfall, 10 to 20 degrees below average. By Wednesday night, \nprecipitation chances start winding down in the far eastern parts \nof the state as the trough sweeps east and takes the moisture \nplume with it.\n\nOne thing to note: Despite the fact the tropical moisture is\ncoming from two tropical cyclones to the south, neither one of\nthese circulations will actually make it up into Arizona. Odalys\nis looping south and will likely die in the open ocean, Polo is \nexpected to make landfall in Baja California, cross the Gulf of \nCalifornia and break apart to our south when it makes landfall \nagain in mainland Mexico. The strong trough and upper-level low \nmoving in from the west and acting on that tropical moisture drawn\nnorth will be the source of the active weather Monday and \nTuesday.\n\nBy Thursday onward dry air from the west and north will take over\nand limit the potential for precipitation through the rest of the\nweek. Some ridging will moving in towards the end of the week \nlook for a pleasant start to October as high temperatures recover \nto a few degrees above average.",
